-- title:
--   Only finitely many lattice points lie in a closed ball
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathbb{Z}^d \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d$ denote the integer lattice, i.e. the set of points all of whose coordinates are integers. For every centre $a \in \mathbb{R}^d$ and every radius $M \in \mathbb{R}$, the set
--   $$\mathbb{Z}^d \cap \overline{B}(a,M)$$
--   is finite.
--
--   This is the local finiteness of the lattice: it makes "the lattice points of a bounded region" a finite set, so that sums over such regions are finite sums and notions such as a block of maximal size are meaningful.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** No hypothesis at all is placed on $a$ or on $M$; in particular $M$ may be negative, in which case the closed ball is empty and the statement is trivially true. Finiteness is `Set.Finite` for a subset of `EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)`.
